{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's diagnostic criterion—divergence between panel and expert on items requiring an expert-unique, outcome-determinative cue—cannot distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance within a single evaluation, because the diagnostic is symmetric with respect to the competing explanation it is meant to separate. If the panel outperforms the expert on expert-unique items, that could mean the checklist genuinely captured the expert's cue-set; but it could equally mean the checklist's mechanical compliance with a surface correlate happens to beat the expert's noisy intuition on those very items. Conversely, if the expert outperforms the panel, that could mean the checklist omitted a genuine cue, or that the expert's intuition is merely tracking a different heuristic that happens to be correlated. The target never identifies what within the single evaluation would break this symmetry: it asserts that the divergence 'reveals whether the checklist has merely tracked the easy cases or genuinely captured the expert's cue-set,' but the divergence pattern alone does not adjudicate between these mechanisms unless one has independent ground truth about which cues are genuinely outcome-determinative versus merely correlated—an external standard the target never requires.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A checklist encodes cue X (a surface correlate that is 90% correlated with the outcome in the evaluation sample). The expert uses cue Y (the true causal driver) but applies it noisily, achieving 60% accuracy. The panel mechanically applies cue X, achieving 85%. On 'expert-unique' items where X and Y diverge, the panel outperforms the expert. SRC_001's diagnostic would read this as the checklist having genuinely captured relevant cues, but it is pure compliance tracking a spurious correlate—the very 'mere checklist compliance' the target claims to distinguish."}],"target":"9461f293d7495ce933fbffad4f708ce8252a25d917cbb1cdc0f36ad058cf07e3"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's OOD-probe requirement is in tension with the problem's constraint of 'a single evaluation.' The target asserts that 'without out-of-distribution probe items, checklist compliance and genuine cue extraction are observationally equivalent,' and demands held-out cases the checklist was not designed for. But within a single evaluation, the OOD probe design itself requires knowledge of the checklist's design distribution to define what counts as OOD—an external standard not available from within the evaluation alone. Moreover, the target's own countercondition acknowledges the problem: if the expert's intuition also fails on OOD probes (both are in-distribution estimators), the divergence is uninformative. This means the OOD diagnostic only works under the narrow condition where the expert generalizes but the panel does not, which presupposes the very thing the evaluation is meant to establish (that the expert has genuine understanding the checklist lacks). The target thus smuggles in a prior about expert generalization capacity that a single evaluation cannot provide, making the diagnostic circular for the claim it is meant to support.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Checklist and expert both achieve 80% on in-distribution items. OOD probes are added; both drop to 55%. Under SRC_002's own countercondition, this divergence is uninformative—yet it is the most common real-world outcome, leaving the target unable to adjudicate the claim in the very scenario it was designed to handle."}],"target":"f8db049e3feda133b7383bd5fb9f13b778ef2aa3e626b04bddb6bb846c5dc31b"}]}